Watching the anime
alone would take you over 17 days straight

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.180
with no sleep and no bathroom breaks.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.220
Just from that alone,
I was really skeptical

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.960
when Netflix announced
that they were making the show.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.420
But after seeing the trailer,
I've got some cautious optimism.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.000
Here's hoping they stick the landing.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.420
But with this Netflix adaptation
coming along and basically asking "what if..."

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.040
That got some cogs turning in my head.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.140
Yeah, what if the One Piece were real?

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.780
And by that I mean,
what if the world of One Piece of a real?

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.000
This is a franchise
with an incredibly unique setup

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.920
in a world
that is instantly recognizable.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.400
So what would happen
if we applied real world science to it?

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.240
That's right, friends,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.460
we're doing a certified theorist
classic today.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.580
If this thing were real,
it would be absolutely terrifying.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.040
Because, make no mistake,
if the world of One Piece were real,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.940
it would be dangerous, disastrous,
and it would ruin basically any attempt

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:42.900
to better your life.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.420
Hoist your Jolly Rogers, loyal theorists,

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.820
we're setting sail.
So before we can really dive

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.860
into why you wouldn't
want to live in the world of One Piece,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.140
we have to break down
just how different it is from our own.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.900
Zooming out, you can immediately spot
some pretty big differences.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.980
First of all, almost the entire planet
is covered in ocean with some small island

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.160
continents and archipelagos dotted here
and there. On the real planet Earth,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.640
with all of our massive continents, about
70% of the planet's surface is water.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.460
So for the world of One Piece,
we're talking closer to 90%.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.800
The biggest landmass is undoubtedly

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.400
the Red Line, a very long, very
skinny continent of red rocky mountain

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.680
jutting out of the ocean,
which runs all along

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.680
what's basically the prime meridian
of the planet. Because of this, One

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.260
Piece's oceans are split
into five main bodies of water.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.600
The four biggest
are the north, south, east and west

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.420
blues basically separated
into the four hemispheres of the planet.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.260
But the fifth body of water is actually
the most important: the Grand Line,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.680
a wide ocean stream that wraps around
the entire equator of the planet.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.840
This stretch of ocean
is incredibly perilous

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.760
and often called
the most dangerous in the world.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.980
Normal compasses don't work here. Currents

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.220
and weather are erratic and extreme,
and much of the wildlife is deadly.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.460
There's a reason its earned itself
the nickname the Pirates Graveyard.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.040
So other than that
forbidding label on the Grand Line,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.560
what would the big issues of this planet
actually be?

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.840
Well, the first problem
the world of One Piece would face

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:55.560
actually comes

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.860
from its most iconic feature: the fact
that it's just covered in ocean water.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.740
See, this one fact would probably mean
that the world's inhabitants

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.900
don't have time to fight over
who's going to get the One Piece first

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.500
because they're too busy fighting over
another, more essential resource, one

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.860
required to survive: water. Uh, fresh water.
I'm sure

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.020
I don't need to tell you, ocean water
not safe to drink.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.240
All that
salt is not going to give you a good time.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:15.900
In the real world,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.740
fresh water only makes up about 2.5%
of the planet's total water supply.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.900
And amongst that already
minuscule percentage,

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.360
most of that is trapped in the atmosphere,
in ice caps,

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.420
or is so deep under Earth's surface
that it won't see the light of day.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.940
The only water that's truly available
for humans to consume

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.300
are those found in underground aquifers
or in freshwater lakes and rivers.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.680
I mean, here's a crazy fact for you:

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.680
if all of Earth's water were shrunk down
to the size of your typical water bottle,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.300
the amount that would be fresh
and available

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.760
would be about a quarter of a milliliter.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.220
That is literally less than a drop
in a bucket.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.220
Now, remember, all of these calculations
are in relation to Earth, a planet

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.860
that has 30% of its surface
covered in land, land

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.720
that has massive lakes and rivers.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.000
And even we still have fresh water
shortages.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.460
Now, take away
all the continents, the Great

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.680
Lakes, the rivers, the streams,
and replace it all

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.740
with even more giant oceans
full of undrinkable saltwater;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.660
people are going to struggle.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.820
Sure, some islands in One Piece
probably have fresh water.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.980
Obviously,
real world islands like those in Hawaii

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.980
have plenty of fresh groundwater
reserves and local spring water.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.360
But those can run dry.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.780
And pumping wells near coastlines
puts you at a massive risk of accidentally

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.620
sucking up ocean water, contaminating
your pool. Factor in the limited farmland

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:20.700
available on the islands

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.640
and you've suddenly created a world
that's going to be forever in conflict,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.780
not over a piece of pirate treasure,
but over a lack of food and water.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.400
The second major environmental problem

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.960
you're going to run into in the world
of One

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.900
Piece is coming from its second
most striking physical feature,

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.840
the Red Line continent
that basically cuts the planet in half.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:37.500
How so?

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.700
Well, this is basically going

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.240
to make a ton of the islands on the planet
borderline impossible to live on.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.160
To explain what I mean,
let's just take a look at this map

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.760
of our very real world, specifically
the West Coast of the United States.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.760
You ever notice how so much of the West
Coast is incredibly green?

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.640
Basically running from Canada
all the way to central California,

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.640
everything is covered in trees
and wildlife. It's beautiful.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.660
And then there's almost a straight line
where everything to the east becomes

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.600
much more brown full of deserts
and dry grasslands.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:04.200
Why has it happened?

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:08.040
Well, that straight line separating the
two regions is the Cascade Mountain Range.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.200
See, there's basically a permanent breeze
flowing

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.920
into North America from the Pacific Ocean
until it reaches the Cascades

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.700
and is suddenly deflected up.
That rapid upward movement forces

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.400
the moisture carried by the wind
to condensate, making a bunch of rain

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.100
and creating an ecosystem
that's fit for a bunch of plant growth.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.240
This is why cities in the Pacific

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.720
Northwest are so famously rainy
and why the forests there are so abundant.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.420
But on the opposite side of the mountain
range, you have the opposite effect.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:31.860
Since all the moisture was taken

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.160
out of the air by the mountains,
the ecosystems are much drier and hotter

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:38.700
on the other side. The phenomenon is
fittingly known as the rain shadow effect

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.800
since the mountains
are casting a metaphorical shadow

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.380
where there's no rain on the far
side of the range.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:44.340
So now let's apply

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.200
what we just talked
about to the world of One Piece.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.620
Since the Red Line is basically a giant
ring of incredibly tall mountains, it's

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.620
going to cast an equally enormous
rain shadow. Since the Grand Line

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.360
ocean current travels from west to east,

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.540
then we would expect the wind
to also be hitting that western side

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.860
of the Red Line mountain range,

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.180
making the eastern side of the Red Lines
devastatingly dry. And wouldn't you know it,

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.260
but that's exactly where our intrepid
straw hat heroes start their adventure.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.040
The first six arcs of One Piece's story
take place in the East Blue Ocean

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.960
and eventually end up at the Red Line.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.420
So this planet, we're even somewhat
realistic,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.000
every single island
they visit at the beginning of the show

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.600
would be an arid desert wasteland
where there just isn't a lot of water

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.100
and rain for life to take hold.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:26.760
And hey, it gets even worse from there
when you take into account the next aspect

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:30.900
of the world of One Piece: the Grand Line.
Or rather, what's around the Grand Line.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:32.280
See, flanking above and below

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.280
the Grand Line is a stretch of sea
known as the Calm Belt. And while that

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.280
might sound like it's going to be smooth
sailing, it actually creates a giant problem.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.220
The Calm Belts here aren't
just expanses of pleasant seas,

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:44.760
they're completely still
and I mean completely still.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:45.570
In contrast to

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:48.840
all the crazy currents and unpredictable
winds of the Grand Line, the Calm Belts

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:52.020
have no wind to catch your sails,
no currents to carry you along.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.780
And that right
there would be outright disastrous,

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.480
maybe even apocalyptic in a real life
climate system.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:01.260
If water and wind just flat out stopped
like it hit a brick wall at the Calm Belt,

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.700
that means that the oceans in the northern

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.800
and southern hemispheres
aren't sharing any water.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.460
Similarly, since the Red Line continent
is basically dividing the world in half,

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.560
it means that the oceans in the West
and eastern

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.420
hemispheres are also not sharing water

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.220
besides the pathetic trickle
that just gets through

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.980
at the reverse mountain.
Basically each ocean in the world of One

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.560
Piece is in its own
entirely enclosed system.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.000
Why is that so bad? Temperature.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.920
Because the planet's a big old spheroid,

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:25.980
the central part of the equator receives
a whole lot more of the sun's heat

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.000
compared to the poles.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.360
That's one of the big reasons that places
like Central America, Central Africa,

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.940
and Southeast Asia have such warm,
tropical climates.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.080
However, the currents

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.800
running throughout our oceans transport
the warmer waters from the equator down

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:41.340
towards the poles, thereby increasing
the overall temperature of those areas

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:42.540
and making them more temperate

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.120
and habitable for more creatures,
including us humans.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:46.920
If you want a really good example
of how this works,

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.000
look no further than Europe.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.640
You ever notice how far north Europe
actually is on the map?

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.080
Cities known for their warmer

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.320
climates like Rome actually sit
on the same latitude as more notoriously

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.160
cold climates

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.560
like Boston.
Europe is able to maintain a warmer

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.600
climate, in part,
thanks to these ocean currents.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.900
So if those currents were to just somehow
stop, large parts of Europe

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:06.960
and North America would likely experience
incredibly long periods

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.620
of freezing conditions, devastating trade
and agriculture in those areas.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.240
Now apply this to the world of One Piece.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.380
The warmer waters inside

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.560
the Grand Line are essentially trapped
inside the Grand Line.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.380
They can't be distributed to the northern
and southern parts of the world.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.900
That would, in essence,
make those parts of the globe

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.900
permanent Arctic wastelands,
unfit for most habitation

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.300
let alone
any tropical island pirate adventures.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:28.800
Now, all of this is awful.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.200
And if One Piece were anything
remotely close to realistic,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.720
this paints
a picture of a devastating hellscape.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.340
Basically, half the world would be desert,

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.080
a lot of the northern
and southern hemispheres

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.480
would be frozen over,
and there would be very few resources

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.700
across the board
to sustain any sort of population.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:42.960
The long and short of it,

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.400
the world of One Piece
is totally messed up.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.800
Not only with the way the world is built,
result in something

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.440
completely unlivable
for a huge percentage of the surface,

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:50.640
but the natural phenomenon

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.880
that we see coming from the Grand Line
don't only make it dangerous for sailing,

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.040
it also completely destroys
any possibility

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.320
of technological advancement
on that planet.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.840
Things would basically be stuck
at the technological level

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:04.380
of the Industrial Revolution or just
before, which hey, wouldn't you know it,

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.180
that's exactly
what we're seeing. In One Piece

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.220
everyone is basically just sailing ships
around.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.880
Engines of any sort are exceedingly rare,
and any sort of transportation

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.160
or technology
usually comes from the use of the weird

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:17.580
wildlife on the planet or magical powers
granted by the Devil Fruit.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:21.000
So in reality when you look at it,
for as scientifically inaccurate

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.940
as this world is the end result is largely
what you're seeing onscreen,

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:27.360
a bunch of people leaving their resource-
starved island in hunt

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.200
of something better.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.080
Hi-diddle-dee-dee a pirate's life for me.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.400
But hey, that's just a theory. A Film Theory!

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:37.260
And cut.

